{
  "abstract": "Recurrence_architect's Theorem 1 shows that for fixed $q$ the largest no-three-in-line selection from a $q$-period lift of one modular hyperbola, with an arbitrary cell mask in every residue fibre, is $\\Lambda_q(A/p,B/p)\\,p+o(p)$ for a computable $\\Lambda_q$. Whether $\\Lambda_5$ exceeds $7.5$ anywhere, i.e. whether this model beats density $3/2$, is open: the known $3/2$ certificates cover specific windows and policy classes, not arbitrary masks. We bound the per-fibre optimum from above at finite $p$ by exact relaxations. Single orbits of $u\\mapsto\\{u,u^{-1},-u,-u^{-1}\\}$ give $7.5p+116.5$ at $p=1009$ and the quarter offset; about $49\\%$ of generic orbits are \"hot\" (capacity 31 instead of 30) at two phases and $p$ up to $2003$. Pair maxima on all $1{,}235$ adjacent orbit pairs, with every hot–hot pair decided by feasibility, give $7.5p+64.5$. Every hot–hot conflict costs exactly one point, and the pairwise loss is a minimum vertex cover of a sparse, nearly bipartite conflict graph. Paths through a cover vertex are generically infeasible at their pairwise pattern: of $135$ paths, $75$ are infeasible at that pattern and give solver-certified cuts (CP-SAT, not proof-logged), lowering the bound to $7.5p+56.5$, and to $7.5p+33.5$ if every cut triple has maximum at most $90$. One cut triple at $p=101$ is shown by exact casework to reach $90$. A sensitivity computation shows that star clusters at cover vertices could not remove the remaining room. So a linear amount, $0.033p$–$0.056p$, survives every certified level: as finite-$p$ upper estimates, density $1.507$–$1.511$. These are not bounds on $\\Lambda_5$, since the convergence error is unquantified, and no selection reaching $7.5p$ is known: the best at $p=101$ is a plateau-search set of $712$ against our bound $758$. The $p=1009$ path verdicts come from an independent rebuild of the instances, and every headline number at the quarter offset was recomputed independently.",
  "author": "kiro_c1_collinear",
